## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of idempotence, per-user isolation, and basic orchestration in Azure Functions using Durable Functions.

## Key Concepts
- Durable Functions orchestrator and fan-out/fan-in
- Idempotent processing using a stable id (blob name)
- Cosmos DB upsert with per-user partitioning
- Service Bus message deduplication via MessageId

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-durable orchestrator sketch
const df = require('durable-functions');
module.exports = df.app();
async function orchestrator(context) {
  const blobName = context.bindingData.name;
  const imageId = blobName; // dedupe key
  // check existing in Cosmos, then upsert
  // fan-out to Resize activities
  const outputs = await context.df.Task.all([
    context.df.callActivity('ResizeA', {imageId}),
    context.df.callActivity('ResizeB', {imageId}),
    context.df.callActivity('ResizeC', {imageId})
  ]);
  await context.df.callActivity('UpsertMetadata', {imageId, userId: context.bindingData.userId});
  await context.df.callActivity('PublishStatus', {imageId});
  return outputs;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures during the fan-out?
- How would you test idempotency across replays without corrupting data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a realistic ingestion path: HTTP input, idempotent storage, and streaming analytics with Azure services, plus the constraints of a Consumption plan.

## Key Concepts
- HTTP trigger in Azure Functions
- Cosmos DB best practices for idempotent keys
- Event Hubs for scalable intake of streams
- Retry/backoff strategies on Consumption plan
- Bindings and error handling in serverless architectures

## Code Example
```javascript
module.exports = async function(context, req) {
  const body = req.body;
  // basic validation
  if (!body?.userId || !body?.eventType || !body?.timestamp) {
    context.res = { status: 400, body: 'Invalid payload' };
    return;
  }
  const id = `${body.userId}|${body.timestamp}`;
  // write summary to Cosmos DB (idempotent key)
  context.bindings.cosmosDoc = { id, userId: body.userId, timestamp: body.timestamp, eventType: body.eventType };
  // publish raw event to Event Hubs
  context.bindings.outputEventHub = body;
  context.res = { status: 202, body: 'Accepted' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate and test idempotency in this flow? 
- What metrics would you observe to ensure latency stays low during bursts?

